{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n2026-09-04, ~21:28-22:09 UTC: the Gerrit host stopped serving entirely for 41 minutes. Root cause\nCONFIRMED; the evidence and its caveats are in the comments.\n\n**The mechanism.** `infra/scripts/observability.sh` re-reads the ENTIRE retained journal on every\nrun with no `--since`, no `--lines` and no `timeout`: four fixed scans (`:760`, `:788`, `:816`,\n`:976`) plus one inside `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` (`:883`) that runs once per marker call\nsite. Measured under gp3-throttled conditions, that exhausts the 300-second timer period at\n~1.37 GB of journal; the host's real journal is 1.7 GB (measured independently by epic 6202 S3).\nControl on the same journal: unbounded 33,936 ms versus `-n 5000` 798 ms, 42.5x. The journald field\nindex does not reduce bytes read.\n\n**The latch.** `infra/scripts/install-observability.sh:20-27` renders a `Type=oneshot` service with\nno `TimeoutStartSec`, so systemd reports `TimeoutStartUSec=infinity`; `:31` sets\n`OnUnitActiveSec=5min`, computed from the last COMPLETED activation. Confirmed on systemd 255: a\nhung run does not delay the timer, it DELETES its next elapse. One overrun silences the probe until\nthe host is rebooted.\n\n**Why it is a defect.** `observability.sh:288-292` states the rule and bounds its own docker calls\nby it — \"a wedged daemon under disk pressure must not hold the 5-minute timer open\". The journal\nscans violate that rule in the same file. And the probe's contract at `:280` / `:487` is that a\nprobe which cannot measure publishes NOTHING, and that its ABSENCE means the host is dead — a probe\nthat can silence itself by hanging inverts its own heartbeat semantics.\n\n**Attribution** (evidence in the attribution comment): `786b-190d-5e3a-4194` created the latch\n(commit `6cd6f4e1f7c0`); `34cd-1a7c-b9b1-4b05` created the scan cost by putting a whole-journal scan\ninside a reusable per-marker helper (commit `e9d4d390c364`).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Terraform may be written and validated, never applied. The live host is not touched.\n- Epic 6202 story S3 is concurrently editing `observability.sh`. Keep the diff tight.\n- Metric names, values and dimensions are fixed by `monitoring*.tf` and must not change.\n- Capping journald is deliberately NOT in scope; S3 owns it and it would not have prevented this.\n  Reasoning is in the scope-note comment, and `user_data.sh` is therefore not in the file impact.\n\n## Repair\n\n### R1 — maintain the cumulative count instead of recomputing it\n\nThe delta design persists a cumulative COUNT and publishes `total - prev`. A cumulative total that\nis RECOMPUTED can only be recomputed by reading from the start of the journal, which is why the\nre-reading is structural. Two wrong repairs are rejected up front: `--since` alone turns `total`\ninto a window count so `total - prev` is garbage; a `timeout` alone yields a silently truncated\ncount so `total - prev` is a plausible wrong number, which the probe's own contract forbids.\n\nSo the total is no longer recomputed, it is MAINTAINED. Each counter persists a journald cursor\nbeside its total; the scan reads only entries after that cursor (`--after-cursor` with\n`--show-cursor`, journald's own exclusive primitive) and adds the new matches to the total. The\npublished delta and every documented behaviour are preserved; only the read volume changes.\n\nState transitions, all four legs named:\n\n- **Cold start** (no state file): seed the cursor from the journal tail, publish 0. Inherited\n  history predates monitoring.\n- **Upgrade** (an existing old-format file holding a bare total and no cursor): treated exactly\n  like a cold start for the cursor — seed from the tail and publish 0 for that run — so the first\n  post-deploy run neither retro-counts the retention window nor performs one last full scan. The\n  existing total is carried forward unchanged, so no delta is fabricated.\n- **Unusable cursor** (journald rotated past it, or it is rejected): the same reseed-from-tail,\n  publishing nothing for that run. An unusable cursor never becomes a permanent stall and never\n  silently degrades to a full scan.\n- **Failure or timeout**: neither cursor nor total advances and nothing is published.\n\nCursor and total are persisted TOGETHER in one state file written temp-file-then-rename, so the\nSIGKILL that R2 introduces cannot leave the pair inconsistent and produce exactly the plausible\nwrong number this repair rejects.\n\nEach scan is additionally wall-clock bounded at 10 seconds, chosen so the bounds nest: 12 scans x\n10 s = 120 s < the 240 s start timeout of R2 < the 300 s timer period. A scan that hits its bound\nis a failure by the rule above, not a truncated count.\n\n### R2 — a finite start timeout, and I/O deprioritisation\n\n`install-observability.sh` renders `TimeoutStartSec=240`, strictly below the 300-second period, so\na hung run is killed before the next elapse. With a finite start timeout the activation always\ncompletes, so `OnUnitActiveSec=5min` re-arms by itself and the latch is removed at its root rather\nthan papered over with a second trigger. The unit also gains `Nice=10` and `IOSchedulingClass=idle`,\nmatching `rebar-autodeploy.service`: the probe is the heavier I/O consumer and currently competes\nwith Gerrit at full priority.\n\n### R3 — the same latch on `rebar-autodeploy.service`\n\nThat unit is also `Type=oneshot` with no `TimeoutStartSec`, driven by `OnUnitActiveSec=2min`. Its\n`git fetch` (`autodeploy.sh:798`) is ALREADY bounded by `timeout \"$FETCH_TIMEOUT\"`; the `git clone`\nat `:787` is not. Either way the oneshot latch applies independently of what the script does, and\none line on the unit removes it.\n\n### R4 — sibling bounds inside the same probe\n\nThe IMDS token/region/instance-id curls, the `git ls-remote` mirror comparison (whose sibling Gerrit\nREST read on the adjacent line correctly uses `--max-time 10`), and the `du` over unexpected\ndata-disk entries all run unbounded in the same 5-minute probe. Each is bounded.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - A steady-state probe run does not read even one full copy of the retained journal.\n      Verified by running the real script under an argument-aware `journalctl` stub that records\n      what it emitted.\n- [ ] AC2 - Journal read volume does not scale with retained-journal size, verified by comparing\n      that recorded volume across two corpora differing 40-fold.\n- [ ] AC3 - Each marker is counted into exactly one interval's delta across three consecutive runs.\n- [ ] AC4 - A journal scan that cannot complete publishes NOTHING for that metric.\n- [ ] AC5 - Such a scan also leaves the counter's persisted state byte-identical.\n- [ ] AC6 - A state file holding an old-format bare total and no cursor publishes 0 on the first\n      run and does not read the retention window.\n- [ ] AC7 - The rendered observability service declares a start timeout strictly less than the\n      period in its own rendered timer, asserted as a relation between the two units.\n- [ ] AC8 - `rebar-autodeploy.service` likewise declares a start timeout below its timer period.\n- [ ] AC9 - Every R4 sibling call terminates when its dependency hangs, verified by driving the\n      script with a hanging stub and asserting the run still completes.\n- [ ] AC10 - The existing observability suite passes, showing the published metric contract is\n      unchanged by the new state representation.\n- [ ] AC11 - Recorded in the tree at the site: bounding disk SPACE does not bound disk IOPS.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nDetection and admission control — a root-volume IOPS alarm, memory-pressure alarming, heterogeneous\ngate admission. This change removes the confirmed cause; a detector must not be built in place of a\nfix. `witted-invisible-roan` owns the memory half."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788566167535799890,"uuid":"7990687f-a692-4001-93e7-a374ff59ff92"}